        "message": "That gives you a very clear, tangible artifact to point to. Two practical ways to handle this: 1. **Ask for an immediate correction on the deck.** If the slides live in a shared workspace (like Google Slides, SharePoint, or a shared drive) or will be sent around as recap notes, tell your coworker: *\"I noticed only your name was on the analysis slide I put together. Let’s update the byline to reflect who wrote it before the final deck is archived or shared further.\"* 2. **Establish ownership in writing with leadership.** If the presentation has already happened and the deck won't be edited, send a brief follow-up to the relevant group or your manager: *\"Following up on [Coworker's] presentation today, here is the background data and methodology behind the analysis on slide [X] that I put together. Happy to answer any questions about the findings.\"* This anchors the work to you with a clear paper trail without turning it into an open confrontation.",
